RG.embAssess = {
  strengths: [
  "It recovers the early church's central conviction that the incarnation and resurrection affirm the body. Irenaeus, Tertullian, Athanasius and Gregory of Nazianzus all argued against Gnostic contempt for matter, and the teaching gives that conviction back to ordinary believers.",
  "It reads Scripture through Jesus. Hebrews 1:3 and John 1:18 make the Son the exact image of the Father, and the teaching refuses any picture of God that contradicts him.",
  "It puts identity before behaviour without excusing sin. Paul does the same, grounding commands in what is already true (Rom 6:11-13; Col 3:1-10; Eph 4:1), and the teaching insists that grace must not feed the appetites (Gal 5:13).",
  "Its account of union is guarded. It keeps the Creator and creature distinct (“you're from the source, not the source”) and rejects New Age self-worship, while taking seriously John 17, Ephesians 5 and 2 Peter 1:4.",
  "It joins theology to the whole person: conscience, nervous system, rest, breathing, relationships and appetites. It speaks to anxiety and shame in a way many believers find freeing, and it rejects the idea that God sends sickness to discipline his children.",
  "It builds a humble, generous church culture: no one judged by status, whoever sees a breach repairs it, honour flows both ways, and the betrayer is still given bread.",
  "It teaches people to read carefully: ask who, to whom, when and where; notice translators' italics; recover worn-out words. Several of its readings are well supported, such as <em>synechō</em> as “hold together,” Hebrews 4:12 as piercing “to the division,” and <em>apalgeō</em> as “past feeling.”"
  ],
  questions: [
  { q: "Is the “biological” and “DNA” language literal?",
  a: "Scripture speaks of new birth (John 3:3), adoption (Gal 4:5), sharing the divine nature (2 Pet 1:4) and being members of Christ's body (1 Cor 12:27), but never of genetics. The teaching says it is resisting “functional docetism” by insisting the change is real, not merely legal, and some of its own phrasing (“your origin has changed but not your material”) shows it does not mean altered chromosomes. Readers do best to hear “DNA” as a strong image for real participation in Christ's life, and to keep the Bible's own words of birth, adoption and union. Note too that the phrase “of his flesh, and of his bones” (Eph 5:30) is absent from many early manuscripts, so it should not carry the weight of an argument alone." },
  { q: "“Inclusive, not substitutionary”: is that a false choice?",
  a: "2 Corinthians 5:14 holds both: “one died for all, then were all dead.” Christ dies <em>for</em> all and all die <em>in</em> him. Many theologians (Athanasius, Torrance) hold inclusion and substitution together: Christ acts in our place precisely as one of us. Texts such as Isaiah 53:5-6, Mark 10:45 and 1 Peter 2:24 use clear “for us” and “in our place” language. The teaching's real target is a picture of an angry Father punishing a reluctant Son. Readers can share that concern while keeping the New Testament's language of Christ dying “for our sins” (1 Cor 15:3)." },
  { q: "Does it focus away from the cross?",
  a: "The teaching says “focusing on the death of Jesus doesn't do anything but destroy your conscience” and urges attention to the resurrection, citing John 16:21. Paul, however, resolved to know “Jesus Christ, and him crucified” (1 Cor 2:2) and gloried in the cross (Gal 6:14), and the Lord's supper proclaims “the Lord's death till he come” (1 Cor 11:26). A fair middle path is that the cross is never seen rightly without Easter, and Easter never without the cross: the problem is preaching the cross as divine rage, not preaching the cross." },
  { q: "Can believers expect not to die, or not to age?",
  a: "The teaching expects decay to reverse (“better at 75 than at 35”) and a generation exempt from death. Scripture does promise incorruption (1 Cor 15:51-54), and Enoch and Elijah were taken (Gen 5:24; 2 Kings 2:11). But Hebrews 9:27 says “it is appointed unto men once to die,” the apostles died, and 2 Corinthians 4:16 speaks of the outward self wasting away while the inner self is renewed. Church tradition also records John's death at Ephesus in old age (Irenaeus, <em>Against Heresies</em> 3.3.4), against the claim that there is no record of it. Teaching on present physical immortality, as in parts of the mid-twentieth-century Latter Rain movement, was widely rejected. It is wiser to hold the hope of resurrection and the reality of present life and health together, without making death a sign of failure." },
  { q: "What about those who are not healed?",
  a: "Scripture records Paul's thorn (2 Cor 12:7-10), Timothy's frequent illness (1 Tim 5:23), Trophimus left sick (2 Tim 4:20) and Epaphroditus near death (Phil 2:27). The teaching's own counsel helps here: one can be “a son of God with a chronic disease,” and asking “am I not his favourite?” is not faith. But lines such as “you are healed, your body will catch up” and claims that most illness is psychosomatic can easily turn into blame. Any healing theology must protect the sick from being told their faith or soul is the problem, and must keep medicine, lament and patient love in view (Rom 12:15)." },
  { q: "“Free to sin” and “the message that addresses behavior comes from a serpent”?",
  a: "Paul anticipated the charge: “Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound? God forbid” (Rom 6:1-2). The teaching means that freedom from fear of the law is the doorway to freedom from sin, and it explicitly warns against using grace as cover for appetite (Gal 5:13) and says God chastens his sons (Heb 12:6). Still, the New Testament does address behaviour, constantly, from Jesus' Sermon on the Mount to Paul's lists in Galatians 5 and Ephesians 4-5. The fair reading is: behaviour is never the root of identity, but it is the fruit Scripture expects and names (Titus 2:11-12)." },
  { q: "Are all the word studies sound?",
  a: "Many are good, but some are not. “Agony” comes from Greek <em>agōn</em> (contest), not from <em>agapē</em>. <em>Orgē</em> means anger or wrath, and the Greek Old Testament of Psalm 95:11 (LXX 94:11) reads “I swore in my wrath”; its “if they shall enter” is a Hebrew oath idiom meaning “they shall not,” so “oh that they would enter” is an unusual rendering. In John 3:2 the Greek is “with him” (<em>met' autou</em>), not “in him.” Ephesians 1:4 uses <em>pro</em> (before), not <em>apo</em>. The verb behind “recapitulation” appears also in Romans 13:9 (“summed up”). The Spirit is grammatically feminine in Hebrew (<em>ruach</em>), neuter in Greek (<em>pneuma</em>), so a figure like “98% feminine” overstates the case. The method the teaching urges, asking what words meant then and there, is the right one; it simply needs applying consistently." },
  { q: "How far does the science support it?",
  a: "Some of it is well grounded: chronic stress affects sleep, immunity and inflammation; slow breathing with longer exhales calms the stress response; purpose, kindness and connection support wellbeing; hematidrosis (sweating blood) is a recognised rare condition. Other claims go beyond the evidence: figures such as “over 90%” or “98%” of illness being psychosomatic are not established medical findings, and the double-slit experiment and Schrödinger's cat do not show that creation arranges itself under human expectation. These are best treated as illustrations, not proofs, and never as reasons to delay medical care." },
  { q: "Is the reading of the flood, Genesis 6 and 1 Enoch reliable?",
  a: "Reading the “sons of God” in Genesis 6 as angelic beings is ancient (1 Enoch, Jude 6-7, 2 Peter 2:4-5, many early fathers), and reading 1 Peter 3:19 as Christ preaching through Noah's generation was held by Augustine. But 1 Enoch is not in the canon of most churches (it is canonical only in the Ethiopian and Eritrean Orthodox churches), and Genesis itself gives human violence and corruption as the reason for the flood (Gen 6:5, 11-13). The teaching's wish to read the flood in the light of Christ is sound; its specific reconstruction is one interpretation among several and should be held loosely." },
  { q: "Do “God is in charge, not in control” and “creation arranges itself under your expectation” go too far?",
  a: "Scripture gives humans real dominion (Gen 1:28; Ps 115:16; Ps 8:6) and links creation's freedom to the revealing of God's children (Rom 8:19-21), so the teaching rightly refuses to make God the author of evil (James 1:13-17). But Scripture also says God works all things toward good (Rom 8:28), that he upholds all things (Heb 1:3), and that he acts freely and personally, not only through human agents. Creation's “subjection” in Romans 8:20 was “by reason of him who hath subjected the same in hope,” God himself. Human authority is real but derived and shared, and expectation is not a law that forces outcomes. Holding both keeps responsibility without turning faith into control." }
  ]
};
